trimmed = """Finding true peace of mind when you rest your head on your pillow happens when you stop carrying guilt and worries that you cannot fix by yourself. You find a clean conscience when you are honest with God about your mistakes, choose to do what is right each day, and rest knowing that He forgives you and cares for your tomorrow. It is a daily gift of calm that comes from living with an open heart and placing your trust in your loving Creator.

### The heavy weight we feel before turning off the light

Have you ever had one of those long, exhausting days where your body is completely tired, but as soon as you turn off the light, your mind starts racing? Anxious thoughts rush in all at once: a sharp word said to someone you love, an unfair situation at work, an unpaid bill on the kitchen counter, or a nagging fear about the days ahead. Your body begs for sleep, but your brain stays wide awake, replaying every mistake like an endless movie in the dark.

In the Bible, in Hebrews 13:18, a man of faith facing trouble and danger wrote with deep honesty: "Pray for us, for we are sure that we have a clear conscience, desiring to act honorably in all things." Notice the simple beauty in what he said. He did not claim to be perfect or better than anyone else. He simply shared that his heart wanted to do what was right, and with great humility, he asked his friends to lift him up in prayer.

A clean conscience does not mean you never make a single mistake. A peaceful conscience begins when you stop hiding your flaws from God and stop pretending to be perfect in front of others. When we mess up, we say sorry from the heart and make things right. When someone hurts our feelings, we let go of anger so bitterness does not poison our soul. Living with honest intentions turns our bed into a safe place of rest rather than a courtroom of guilt and self-blame.

### A loving Shepherd who stays awake while you sleep

What should we do when our thoughts keep accusing us of past mistakes that we can no longer fix? Here we discover the greatest relief for our tired souls. The Bible calls God the "God of peace," reminding us that He brought back from the dead our Lord Jesus, calling Him "the great Shepherd of the sheep" (Hebrews 13:20).

Think of a shepherd watching over his sheep in an open field. During the bright hours of the day, he gently leads them toward green grass and cool water. When night falls, the sheep can sleep in quiet safety because their shepherd sits at the gate, keeping watch against wolves and hidden dangers. Jesus does this very thing for our lives every day. He gave His life to wash away our wrongs and lift the crushing burden of guilt off our shoulders.

The Bible calls this wonderful gift grace: unconditional love and favor that none of us could ever buy or earn, but which God gives freely when we trust Him. When this kindness fills your heart, guilt loses its power over your nights. You do not have to climb into bed making excuses for where you fell short today. The One who protects your life never grows tired and never sleeps. You can close your eyes in deep peace, knowing that God is in full control of everything that has slipped out of your hands.

### No one was ever made to carry the world alone

Another reason we often toss and turn at night is the quiet, painful feeling that we are totally alone in our daily battles. We start believing that everything depends on our own strength, and we try to carry the whole world on our shoulders. Near the end of his letter, the writer shares warm news about his dear friend Timothy, sends greetings from other believers, and asks everyone to stay close in love (Hebrews 13:22-24). He understood that nobody was created to walk through this life all by themselves.

Worries become heaviest in the quiet dark when we spend all day keeping our hurt locked inside and pretending to be strong. Talking to a trustworthy friend or pouring out your heart to God brings instant relief to an overloaded mind. You do not need big words or fancy speeches to talk to God. Prayer is simply an honest conversation with someone who loves you—just like sitting at a kitchen table with a warm cup of tea, opening up to a best friend who truly listens.

The closing blessing of the letter wraps around our tired spirits like a warm blanket: "Grace be with all of you" (Hebrews 13:25). That grace is God’s gentle hand holding you up tonight and promising that tomorrow morning He will be right beside you, ready with fresh strength for whatever comes your way.

### Your practical next step for tonight

To taste this peace and lay your head down with a quiet mind tonight, try following this simple bedtime routine before you turn out the lights:

1. **Turn down the noise:** About fifteen minutes before climbing into bed, switch off the television and place your phone across the room. Give your mind a quiet window to slow down from the rush of the day.
2. **Pray with simple honesty:** Sit quietly on your bed, take a deep breath, and talk to God in your own natural words. Tell Him what feels heavy, ask Him to forgive your mistakes from today, and choose to forgive anyone who hurt you. If there is a chore you must handle tomorrow, write it down on a pad of paper and leave it there until morning.
3. **Hand it over to the Great Shepherd:** Speak these words from your heart: "Lord, God of peace, I did the best I could today. Now I place all my worries into Your caring hands. You watch over me while I sleep, and I choose to rest in Your grace."
4. **Welcome restful sleep:** Lie down with the comforting promise that God loves you dearly, your slate is clean in His sight, and tomorrow brings brand-new mercies and a fresh start."""

words = trimmed.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
